\section{Balance Clock Intervals, Not Source Lines}

One cycle must accommodate the slowest path between sequential boundaries, including register overhead. Putting a register after a fixed number of source operators is not a useful metric: a multiply, a mux, and a wire do not take the same time. Not all delays even come from operators; a short expression can drive a long, heavily loaded net. Balance is about the delay between actual endpoints, measured after mapping and revisited after routing.

Pipelining introduces clock boundaries, often changing interface latency while keeping or improving throughput. Retiming instead redistributes existing registers in a synchronous graph. If the register counts on input-to-output paths are to stay fixed, the interface vertices must be held at the same retiming offset. At forks and joins, a move must be applied to the related edges, not just to a convenient long path. Kasprzyk and colleagues' datapath IR makes data and predicates explicit; the same discipline is essential below the IR, where a predicate can be a valid flag or a selector that must arrive with its matching data.

