\section{Move One Boundary and Carry the Valid Bit}

Let a feed-forward path consist of combinational vertices \(A,B,C\), with nominal delays \(4,3,2\) ns. The original internal register is after \(B\), so the intervals are \(7\) and \(2\) ns. Move it before \(B\), and they are \(4\) and \(5\) ns. If each interval has another \(1\) ns of clock-to-Q plus setup and no skew, the illustrative minimum setup period falls from \(8\) to \(6\) ns. This is an abstract calculation, not a characterization of the operators in the example. \Cref{fig:visual-315} shows what does not change: there are still two clock intervals from the launch register to the output register.

An example of the revised schedule in SystemVerilog \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}, with eight-bit modular operations and no backpressure:

\begin{verbatim}
logic [7:0] mid, y;
logic vmid, vout;
always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
    if (rst) begin
        vmid <= 1'b0;
        vout <= 1'b0;
    end else begin
        vmid <= vin;
        vout <= vmid;
        mid <= x + 8'd3;                  // A
        y <= (mid ^ 8'h55) + 8'd7;        // C(B(mid))
    end
end
\end{verbatim}

The contract checks \(y\) only when \texttt{vout} is high. At an edge accepting \(x=254\), \(A\) gives \(1\); the next edge gives \(C(B(1))=84+7=91\). Reset clears both valid flags and cancels any pending observation. If reset must also set the visible payload irrespective of valid, it is not sufficient to ignore it in the implementation or in a miter.

Graph retiming pseudocode, with \(w(u,v)\) the original register count on an edge and integer \(r(v)\) a retiming offset:

\begin{verbatim}
r = choose_offsets_with_fixed_io()       # anchors at the interface
for each edge (u, v):
    moved[u,v] = w[u,v] + r[v] - r[u]    # counts, not time units
    require moved[u,v] >= 0
require max_zero_register_path_delay() <= target
require equivalent_reset_and_observations() # beyond graph legality
\end{verbatim}

For the example choose \(r(B)=1\) and all other offsets zero. The original edge counts \(w(A,B)=0,\ w(B,C)=1\) become \(1,0\). Around a directed cycle all offsets cancel, so the total number of registers on that cycle is invariant; they cannot be removed from the recurrence by retiming. Along an input-to-output path only the endpoint offsets remain, and the fixed endpoints preserve the count there too.

