\section{Where the Graph Model Stops Being Enough}

The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/cmd/abc.html}{Yosys ABC command reference} distinguishes its normal combinational invocation from \texttt{abc -dff}, which can pass supported flip-flops to ABC and partitions clock domains. An enabled sequential flow is not a license to move a register through asynchronous resets, clock-domain crossings, RAM ports, or user-observable protocol state. Even within one clock domain, imitating an initial state after a move can require an initialization value that has no inverse image through the intervening function.

For stalls, the data, selector, and valid registers must advance under compatible enables or be managed by a protocol designed to preserve order despite independent stalls. Shortened paths can also expose hold problems. Neither the graph invariant nor a sequential equivalence proof checks placement or electrical timing, so repeat static timing for maximum and minimum delay. If an indivisible hard primitive or a feedback cycle sets the bottleneck, further improvement may need a different algorithm or a new interface contract, not another local register move.
